### What reaches the build context, stated as an allow-list.
#
# A deny-list is the wrong shape for this repository. The build context is the repository root, which is also where a
# developer's `.env`, a mounted secret, a certificate, and an agent worktree live, and every one of those would be sent
# to the daemon by a rule that only names what it remembers to exclude. Excluding everything first means a file reaches
# the build because a line below says it does.
#
# The build stage needs the sources and the shared build contract; nothing here decides what reaches the *runtime*
# image, which copies the publish output and nothing else.

**

!/src
!/Directory.Build.props
!/Directory.Packages.props
!/NuGet.config
!/global.json

# `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild` and `TreatWarningsAsErrors` are both on, so the analyzer severities decide whether the
# build succeeds at all. Without these two the container build would apply default severities and fail on diagnostics
# the repository has configured away — or, worse, pass while the repository's own gate does not.
!/.editorconfig
!/.config/BannedSymbols.txt

# The project's own license and attribution. `Host` publishes both beside the assemblies and fails the publish when
# either is absent, so an allow-list that forgot them would stop this build rather than quietly ship an image without
# them. They are what a recipient of a pulled image reads; the `org.opencontainers.image.licenses` label only names
# the same terms in a form a registry can index.
!/LICENSE
!/NOTICE

# Nothing under deploy/ is re-included, deliberately. The Dockerfile beside this file is read from the host through
# `--file` rather than out of the context, and the Compose file, the chart, and the example configuration are read by
# an operator's tooling rather than baked into an image.

# Build output from the host machine. It is architecture- and configuration-specific, it can be stale, and letting it
# into the context would let a local build decide what the image contains.
/src/**/bin
/src/**/obj
